\section{Introduction and statement of results}\label{sec:introduction}

Alp\"oge has announced a compact complex threefold $X$, diffeomorphic to $S^6$, obtained by completing a family of complex two-tori over a thrice-punctured projective line~\cite{Alpoge}.  This paper studies the differential geometry carried by the smooth torus-fibred locus
\[
 f^\circ:X^\circ\longrightarrow B^\circ,
 \qquad B^\circ=\PP^1\setminus\{p_0,p_1,p_2\}.
\]
The principal result is six-dimensional: the period data determine a semi-flat Ricci-flat pseudo-K\"ahler metric with holonomy contained in $SU(1,2)$.  The associated split-$G_2$ structure on $X^\circ\times S^1$ is a useful equivalent formulation, but it is cylindrical and has a parallel circle direction.

The mechanism is most transparent in the intersection space
\[
 H^2(\TT^4,\RR),
\]
whose wedge pairing has signature $(3,3)$.  Two features of Alp\"oge's construction interact:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*),leftmargin=2em]
 \item a flat monodromy-invariant class $\eta$ of positive wedge norm, represented by a fibrewise pseudo-K\"ahler form of signature $(1,1)$;
 \item a nowhere-vanishing holomorphic three-form $\Xi$ on $X^\circ$, whose contraction along the base gives an $H^2$-valued holomorphic one-form $\alpha$.
\end{enumerate}
The fibrewise $(2,0)$ form underlying $\alpha$ is decomposable, hence null for the wedge pairing.  It is orthogonal to $\eta$, and the sign of its Hermitian norm is negative.  Consequently the real three-plane generated by $\eta$, $\re\alpha$, and $\im\alpha$ has signature $(1,2)$.  This single signature calculation drives both constructions in the paper: it produces the $SU(1,2)$ normalization directly and turns a primitive of $\alpha$ into Weierstrass data for a minimal immersion in $\RR^{3,3}$.

\begin{theorem}[Main theorem]\label{thm:main}
Conditional on Alp\"oge's announced construction, the smooth locus $X^\circ$ has the following properties.
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*),leftmargin=2em]
 \item There is a fibrewise translation-invariant torsion-free $SU(1,2)$ structure $(\omega,\Xi)$ on $X^\circ$.  Its metric is pseudo-K\"ahler and Ricci-flat of real signature $(2,4)$; the restriction to each smooth torus fibre is flat of signature $(2,2)$.
 \item On the universal cover $\widetilde B^\circ$, the period one-form has a holomorphic primitive $\cF$, and
 \[
   U(r,z)=\frac{r}{\sqrt6}\eta+\re\cF(z)
   :\RR\times\widetilde B^\circ\longrightarrow H^2(\TT^4,\RR)\cong\RR^{3,3}
 \]
 is a linearly full minimal immersion with induced signature $(1,2)$.
 \item The form
 \[
   \varphi=\dd s\wedge\omega+\re\Xi
 \]
 defines a torsion-free split-$G_2$ structure on $X^\circ\times S^1$.  Baraglia's semi-flat construction applied to $U$ yields the same local metric and stable form, up to the conventional overall sign of $\varphi$.
 \item The scalar
 \[
  D=\im\beta-\frac{6(\im\mu)^2}{\im\tau}
 \]
 is a real-form discriminant: its sign determines whether the period three-plane gives the compact or split semi-flat geometry, while $D=0$ is exactly the real-lattice degeneration locus.  For Alp\"oge's family $D<0$ throughout $B^\circ$.  The resulting $SU(1,2)$ structure cannot extend smoothly over compact $X$, and the cylindrical split-$G_2$ structure cannot extend over $X\times S^1$ while preserving a non-null parallel circle direction.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

